# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Hosea'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'Israel the Unfaithful Wife, and the LORD’s Faithful Love'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Hosea is “the word of the LORD that came unto Hosea” in the days of the kings of Judah and Jeroboam king of Israel (Hos. 1:1). The prophet’s own marriage becomes a picture of the LORD and Israel. The book speaks of the LORD’s controversy with the land, of Israel called out of Egypt, of days without a king, of a return “in the latter days,” and of healing and love freely given (Hos. 3:4–5; 4:1; 11:1; 14:4).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to the children of Israel and to Ephraim about their unfaithfulness, their judgment and their return (Hos. 1:10; 3:5; 4:1; 14:1).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Hosea', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Prophet’s Marriage and the Children (Chs. 1–3)'), ('p', 'Hosea’s family speaks of Israel’s state, the LORD promises to betroth her for ever, and Israel is to abide many days without a king (Hos. 1:10; 2:19–20; 3:4–5).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Controversy With the Land (Chs. 4–8)'), ('p', 'The LORD pleads with Israel for lack of knowledge and truth (Hos. 4:1, 6; 5:15).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Judgment and Mercy (Chs. 9–11)'), ('p', 'Ephraim is judged, yet the LORD asks how He can give him up (Hos. 6:6; 11:1, 8–9).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Redemption Promised (Ch. 13)'), ('p', 'The LORD will ransom from the power of the grave (Hos. 13:14).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Return and Healing (Ch. 14)'), ('p', 'Israel is told to return, and the LORD promises to heal their backsliding (Hos. 14:1, 4, 9).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Hosea', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Numberless Israel</b> — “The number of the children of Israel shall be as the sand of the sea” (Hos. 1:10).', '<b>Betrothed for Ever</b> — “I will betroth thee unto me for ever” (Hos. 2:19).', '<b>Days Without a King</b> — “The children of Israel shall abide many days without a king, and without a prince, and without a sacrifice” (Hos. 3:4).', '<b>David Their King</b> — “Afterward shall the children of Israel return, and seek the LORD their God, and David their king” (Hos. 3:5).', '<b>Lack of Knowledge</b> — “My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge” (Hos. 4:6).', '<b>Out of Egypt</b> — “When Israel was a child, then I loved him, and called my son out of Egypt” (Hos. 11:1).', '<b>Love Freely Given</b> — “I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely” (Hos. 14:4).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Hosea', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Hosea points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>Called Out of Egypt</b> — “Called my son out of Egypt” (Hos. 11:1) is quoted in the Gospel of Matthew (Matt. 2:15).', '<b>Mercy Not Sacrifice</b> — “I desired mercy, and not sacrifice” (Hos. 6:6) is quoted by the Lord (Matt. 9:13; 12:7).', '<b>Ransomed From the Grave</b> — “I will ransom them from the power of the grave” (Hos. 13:14); Paul’s words in 1 Corinthians 15:55 are reported as written beside it.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Hosea', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Hosea speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Hosea operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Hosea in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Hosea reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the children of Israel as the covenant people addressed (Hos. 1:10; 4:1)', 'the LORD’s betrothal of Israel for ever (Hos. 2:19)', 'days without king, sacrifice or image, and a return in the latter days (Hos. 3:4–5)', 'the LORD’s controversy with the land and Ephraim (Hos. 4:1; 11:8)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Hosea that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Hosea contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['A Numberless Israel — “the number of the children of Israel shall be as the sand of the sea.” (Hos. 1:10).', 'Betrothed for Ever — “I will betroth thee unto me for ever.” (Hos. 2:19).', 'Days Without a King — “the children of Israel shall abide many days without a king, and without a prince, and without a sacrifice.” (Hos. 3:4).', 'The Latter Days — “Afterward shall the children of Israel return, and seek the LORD their God, and David their king.” (Hos. 3:5).', 'The Controversy — “the LORD hath a controversy with the inhabitants of the land.” (Hos. 4:1).', 'Lack of Knowledge — “My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge.” (Hos. 4:6).', 'Seeking His Face — “till they acknowledge their offence, and seek my face.” (Hos. 5:15).', 'The Son Out of Egypt — “When Israel was a child, then I loved him, and called my son out of Egypt.” (Hos. 11:1).', 'Ransom From the Grave — “I will ransom them from the power of the grave.” (Hos. 13:14).', 'Return, O Israel — “O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God.” (Hos. 14:1).', 'Healed and Loved — “I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely.” (Hos. 14:4).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Hosea Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Hosea and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Hosea teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Hosea', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['A Numberless Israel', '“the number of the children of Israel shall be as the sand of the sea.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 1:10'], ['Betrothed for Ever', '“I will betroth thee unto me for ever.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 2:19'], ['Days Without a King', '“the children of Israel shall abide many days without a king, and without a prince, and without a sacrifice.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 3:4'], ['The Latter Days', '“Afterward shall the children of Israel return, and seek the LORD their God, and David their king.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 3:5'], ['The Controversy', '“the LORD hath a controversy with the inhabitants of the land.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 4:1'], ['Lack of Knowledge', '“My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 4:6'], ['Seeking His Face', '“till they acknowledge their offence, and seek my face.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 5:15'], ['The Son Out of Egypt', '“When Israel was a child, then I loved him, and called my son out of Egypt.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 11:1'], ['Ransom From the Grave', '“I will ransom them from the power of the grave.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 13:14'], ['Return, O Israel', '“O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 14:1'], ['Healed and Loved', '“I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hos. 14:4']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Hosea that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Hosea teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Mercy and Knowledge', '“I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings.”', 'Hos. 6:6'], ['God and Not Man', '“I am God, and not man; the Holy One in the midst of thee.”', 'Hos. 11:9'], ['The Ways of the LORD', '“the ways of the LORD are right, and the just shall walk in them.”', 'Hos. 14:9']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Hosea without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Hosea in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Desire mercy</b> — The LORD desired mercy and the knowledge of God (Hos. 6:6).', '<b>Know He is God and not man</b> — The Holy One in the midst (Hos. 11:9).', '<b>Walk in His right ways</b> — The just shall walk in them (Hos. 14:9).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Hosea', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Hosea in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Prophet’s Marriage (Chs. 1–3) — Betrothed for ever. <i>(Hos. 1:10; 2:19; 3:4–5)</i>', 'The Controversy With the Land (Chs. 4–8) — Lack of knowledge. <i>(Hos. 4:1, 6; 5:15)</i>', 'Judgment and Mercy (Chs. 9–11) — Out of Egypt. <i>(Hos. 6:6; 11:1, 9)</i>', 'Redemption Promised (Ch. 13) — Ransom from the grave. <i>(Hos. 13:14)</i>', 'Return and Healing (Ch. 14) — Loved freely. <i>(Hos. 14:1, 4, 9)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Hosea, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward repentance, knowledge of God, and trust in His mercy without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Hosea simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Hosea stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
